{"type": "spr", "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In right triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>X</mi>\n\t<mi>Y</mi>\n\t<mi>Z</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, angles <math alttext=\"upper X\"><mi>X</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> are acute angles. Point <math alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi>\n</math> lies on <math alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> such that <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper R\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is perpendicular to <math alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>. The measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper X upper Z upper Y\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"a degree\"><mi>a</mi>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math>, the measure of <math alttext=\"angle upper X upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"b degree\"><mi>b</mi>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math>, and the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Y\"><mover><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn>\n</math>. If&nbsp;<math alttext=\"sine a degree  equals StartFraction 12 Over 13 EndFraction\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>13</mn></mfrac></math>, what is the value of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"tangent b degree\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>?</p>", "keys": ["2.4", "12/5"], "rationale": "<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 12 Over 5 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>. It's given that in right triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Z\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Z</mi></math> are acute angles. It follows that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Y\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Y</mi></math> is a right angle. It's given that point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math> lies on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Z\"><menclose notation=\"top\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></menclose></math> such that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper R\"><menclose notation=\"top\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></menclose></math> is perpendicular to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Z\"><menclose notation=\"top\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></menclose></math>. Since&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Y\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Y</mi></math> is a right angle and is comprised of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Z upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math>, it follows that the sum of the measures of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Z upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"90 degree\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's given that the measure of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b degree\"><mi>b</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that the measure of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Z upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 90 minus b right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Since&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper R\"><menclose notation=\"top\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></menclose></math> is perpendicular to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper X upper Z\"><menclose notation=\"top\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></menclose></math>, it follows that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X upper R upper Y\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Z upper R upper Y\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> are right angles. It follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"increment upper X upper R upper Y\"><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"increment upper Z upper R upper Y\"><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> are right triangles. It's given that the measure of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X upper Z upper Y\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a degree\"><mi>a</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Because the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, it follows that the measures of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Z upper R upper Y\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Y</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X upper Z upper Y\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>Y</mi></math>, and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Z upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> have a sum of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.&nbsp;Since the measure of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Z upper R upper Y\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"90 degree\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, the measure of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Y upper Z upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a degree\"><mi>a</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, and the measure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Z upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 90 minus b right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, it follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"90 plus a plus 90 minus b equals 180\"><mn>90</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a minus b plus 180 equals 180\"><mi>a</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>. Subtracting&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180\"><mn>180</mn></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a minus b equals 0\"><mi>a</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Adding&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals b\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></math>. It's given that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"sine a degree  equals StartFraction 12 Over 13 EndFraction\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>13</mn></mfrac></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals b\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></math>, it follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"sine b degree  equals StartFraction 12 Over 13 EndFraction\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>13</mn></mfrac></math>. In a right triangle, the sine of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. It follows that, for a positive constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"12 k\"><mn>12</mn><mi>k</mi></math> represents the length of the leg opposite&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"13 k\"><mn>13</mn><mi>k</mi></math> represents the length of the hypotenuse of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"triangle upper X upper R upper Y\"><mo>&#9651;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Y</mi></math>. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.&nbsp;Let&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"n k\"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></math> represent the length of the leg adjacent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math>. It follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 13 k right parenthesis squared equals left parenthesis 12 k right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis n k right parenthesis squared\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>13</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"169 k squared equals 144 k squared plus n squared k squared\"><mn>169</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>144</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.&nbsp;Subtracting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"144 k squared\"><mn>144</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"25 k squared equals n squared k squared\"><mn>25</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5 k equals n k\"><mn>5</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></math>. Therefore, the length of the leg adjacent to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper X upper Y upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5 k\"><mn>5</mn><mi>k</mi></math>. In a right triangle, the tangent of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. It follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"tangent b degree  equals StartFraction 12 k Over 5 k EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>12</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"tangent b degree  equals StartFraction 12 Over 5 EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>. Note that 12/5&nbsp;and 2.4&nbsp;are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>", "externalid": "7dae086f-c99c-48f6-b4ad-68e5e1008035", "correct_answer": ["2.4", "12/5"]}